  show    Shows one draft by its draft id (`gmail.readonly` is enough; mirrors the `gmail_draft_show` MCP tool)
  create  Creates a draft to review and send from Gmail (needs `gmail.modify`; CLI-only)
  update  Updates a draft in place, keeping its id and thread (needs `gmail.modify`; CLI-only)
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi gmail draft list =====
Lists drafts with their draft ids (`gmail.readonly` is enough; mirrors the `gmail_draft_list` MCP tool)

Usage: gwi gmail draft list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --query <QUERY>
          Only list drafts matching this Gmail search query (same syntax as the Gmail search box, e.g. `to:alice subject:report`)

      --limit <LIMIT>
          Maximum drafts to return. `0` means "every draft" (capped at the same hard ceiling as `gmail search`)
          
          [default: 50]

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi gmail draft show =====
Shows one draft by its draft id (`gmail.readonly` is enough; mirrors the `gmail_draft_show` MCP tool)

Usage: gwi gmail draft show [OPTIONS] <DRAFT_ID>

Arguments:
  <DRAFT_ID>
          Gmail draft id (the `DRAFT_ID` column of `gmail draft list`)

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --out-file <PATH>
          Output file (writes to stdout if omitted). With `--detail raw`, the message's exact RFC 2822 bytes, i.e. an `.eml` file

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --detail <DETAIL>
          How much of the message to fetch

          Possible values:
          - minimal:  Only `id`/`threadId`/`labelIds`/`sizeEstimate` — no headers or body
          - metadata: Headers and snippet only, no body
          - full:     The full parsed MIME structure. Default
          - raw:      The full RFC 2822 message, base64url-encoded
          
          [default: full]

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table:    Human-readable table (id/thread-id/labels/snippet). Default
          - json:     JSON
          - yaml:     YAML (single document)
          - yamls:    YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl:    JSON Lines
          - markdown: Human-readable Markdown rendering of the full message (headers + body). Always fetches the complete raw MIME message regardless of `--detail`, since rendering needs the full message structure
          
          [default: table]

      --fold-quotes
          Collapses `>`-quoted reply history nested more than one level deep into a one-line `*(N quoted lines omitted)*` marker (rust-works/omni-dev#1514). Only affects `-o markdown`, mirroring `--detail`'s reverse asymmetry (it is silently ignored elsewhere). Off by default: verbatim rendering is fully information-preserving, and the full text is one re-render away without this flag

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi gmail draft create =====
Creates a draft to review and send from Gmail (needs `gmail.modify`; CLI-only)

Usage: gwi gmail draft create [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --from <ADDR>
          Send as this address: one of the account's verified send-as aliases, or its primary address, as `addr@example.com` or `"Name <addr@example.com>"`. Without a name, Gmail's name for the address is used

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --to <ADDR>...
          A `To` recipient: `addr@example.com` or `"Name <addr@example.com>"`. Repeat the flag (or list several after it) for more recipients. Required unless `--reply-to` is given, which defaults it from the original

      --cc <ADDR>...
          A `Cc` recipient, in the same form as `--to`

      --bcc <ADDR>...
          A `Bcc` recipient, in the same form as `--to`

      --subject <SUBJECT>
          The subject. Required unless `--reply-to` is given, which defaults it to `Re: <original subject>`

      --body <BODY>
          The plain-text body

      --body-file <PATH>
          Read the plain-text body from this UTF-8 file

      --html-body <HTML>
          An HTML body, sent alongside the plain-text one. Without `--body` or `--body-file`, the plain text is derived from the HTML

      --html-body-file <PATH>
          Read the HTML body from this UTF-8 file

      --attach <PATH>...
          Attach a file. Repeat the flag (or list several after it) for more

      --reply-to <MESSAGE_ID>
          Reply to this Gmail message id, filing the draft in its thread. Without `--to`, replies to the original's `Reply-To`, else its `From` (or, for a message you sent, its `To`)

      --reply-all
          With `--reply-to`, also address the original's other `To` and `Cc` recipients, leaving out your own addresses. `--to`/`--cc` still replace the defaults for their header

      --raw <FILE>
          Upload this complete RFC 5322 message (`.eml`) byte for byte instead of composing one

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi gmail draft update =====
Updates a draft in place, keeping its id and thread (needs `gmail.modify`; CLI-only)

Usage: gwi gmail draft update [OPTIONS] <--from <ADDR>|--to <ADDR>|--cc <ADDR>|--bcc <ADDR>|--subject <SUBJECT>|--body <BODY>|--body-file <PATH>|--html-body <HTML>|--html-body-file <PATH>|--attach <PATH>|--remove-attachment <NAME>|--raw <FILE>> <DRAFT_ID>

Arguments:
  <DRAFT_ID>
          Gmail draft id (the `DRAFT_ID` column of `gmail draft list`)

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --from <ADDR>
          Send as this address: one of the account's verified send-as aliases, or its primary address, as `addr@example.com` or `"Name <addr@example.com>"`. Without a name, Gmail's name for the address is used

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --to <ADDR>
          Replace the `To` recipients: `addr@example.com` or `"Name <addr@example.com>"`. Repeat the flag for more recipients

      --cc <ADDR>
          Replace the `Cc` recipients, in the same form as `--to`

      --bcc <ADDR>
          Replace the `Bcc` recipients, in the same form as `--to`

      --subject <SUBJECT>
          Replace the subject

      --body <BODY>
          Replace the body with this plain text (with `--html-body`, its plain-text version)

      --body-file <PATH>
          Replace the body with the plain text in this UTF-8 file

      --html-body <HTML>
          Replace the body with this HTML and a plain-text version of it: the `--body` text when given, else one derived from the HTML

      --html-body-file <PATH>
          Replace the body with the HTML in this UTF-8 file, as `--html-body`

      --attach <PATH>
          Attach a file after the existing attachments. Repeat the flag for more

      --remove-attachment <NAME>
          Remove the attachment `gmail draft show` lists under this name (or the one stored under it, when only one is). Repeat the flag for more

      --raw <FILE>
          Replace the whole message with this RFC 5322 file (`.eml`), uploaded byte for byte

      --if-message-id <ID>
          Refuse the update unless the draft's current message id is this one, e.g. the `MESSAGE_ID` from when you last read the draft

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi gmail sync =====
Maintains a durable local archive of a mailbox (CLI-only; no MCP equivalent)

Usage: gwi gmail sync [OPTIONS] --output-dir <PATH>

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --output-dir <PATH>
          Directory to maintain the archive in. Created if missing

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --query <QUERY>
          Restrict the archive to messages matching this Gmail search query (same syntax as `gmail search --query`). Only applied on backfill/`--full`/reconciliation passes — an incremental sync's `history.list` has no query filter, so newly-arrived mail matching the query is only picked up by a later `--full` re-run (see docs/gmail.md's Sync section)

      --exclude-label <LABEL_ID>
          Excludes messages carrying this label id from the archive (rust-works/omni-dev#1780). Repeatable — e.g. `--exclude-label SPAM --exclude-label TRASH`. Applied fully and generally on incremental passes (no extra API calls; a `messagesAdded` history event matching this filter is never fetched, and a `labelsAdded`/`labelsRemoved` event that crosses the excluded boundary soft-deletes/undeletes the archived record). On backfill/`--full`/reconciliation passes, only entries with a known query translation take effect (currently `SPAM`/`TRASH` — folded into the listing query as `-in:spam`/`-in:trash`); anything else still filters future incremental runs, but won't be excluded retroactively on this pass unless you also pass `--query`

      --full
          Forces a full backfill/reconciliation pass even if a valid watermark exists

      --retry-pending
          Retries every message still pending from an earlier failed fetch now, ignoring the retry backoff that would otherwise defer it (rust-works/omni-dev#1790). Use after fixing whatever made the fetches fail. A retry that fails again still counts toward — and restarts — that message's backoff

      --concurrency <CONCURRENCY>
          Bounds concurrent message fetches. Clamped to `1..=gmail::messages_api::MAX_CONCURRENCY`
          
          [default: 20]

      --dry-run
          Reports the planned actions without writing any files

      --extract-attachments
          Also writes each message's attachment MIME parts to disk as separate files under `<eml-shard-dir>/<id>/attachments/<filename>`, alongside the existing `.eml`. Off by default: extraction is additional I/O/disk usage per message, and the `.eml` stays the lossless source of truth regardless. Only applies to messages actually fetched this run — presence-on-disk still skips an already-archived message, so turning this on does not retroactively backfill an existing archive (delete the affected `.eml` files, or the whole archive, and re-run `--full` to force re-extraction)

      --quiet
          Only shows errors/warnings, suppresses info-level output — including the live progress bars a backfill/`--full`/reconciliation pass shows on an interactive terminal (rust-works/omni-dev#1502)

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Report format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi gmail sync-all =====
Maintains durable local archives for every account in `.gwi/gmail-sync.yaml`, concurrently (CLI-only; no MCP equivalent; ADR-0068)

Usage: gwi gmail sync-all [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Gmail account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Gmail account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066). Overrides `GWI_GMAIL_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `gmail` subtree — unlike `--profile` it is not usable before the `gmail` subcommand name, only after it (`gmail --account NAME <cmd>` or `gmail <cmd> --account NAME`), so it can't collide with another product's own `--account` flag (Drive has the same one) as more command trees are added.

      --context-dir <PATH>
          Overrides the standard `.gwi/` discovery (walk-up from CWD, `GWI_CONFIG_DIR`, `local/` shadow) used to locate `gmail-sync.yaml`

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --concurrency <N>
          Overrides `gmail-sync.yaml`'s `concurrency` — a fetch-request cap shared across every configured account this run. Left unset, the YAML value applies; if that's unset too, falls back to the same default as `gmail sync --concurrency`

      --full
          Forces a full backfill/reconciliation pass for every account, same as `gmail sync --full`

      --retry-pending
          Retries every account's pending failed fetches now, ignoring their retry backoff, same as `gmail sync --retry-pending` (rust-works/omni-dev#1790)

      --dry-run
          Reports the planned actions for every account without writing any files

      --quiet
          Suppresses the per-account summary line printed as each account finishes, and the live per-account progress bars a run shows on an interactive terminal; per-message errors and the trailing combined total still print regardless

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Report format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

