===== gwi drive sheets find-replace =====
Finds text and replaces it across a range, one sheet, or the workbook. Gated by `sheets-write` (issue rust-works/omni-dev#1841, ADR-0083 §1)

Usage: gwi drive sheets find-replace [OPTIONS] --find <TEXT> --replacement <TEXT> <SPREADSHEET_ID>

Arguments:
  <SPREADSHEET_ID>
          Spreadsheet id (the /d/<ID>/ segment of a Sheets URL).

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Drive account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Drive account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066, ADR-0069). Overrides `GWI_DRIVE_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `drive` subtree — not usable before the `drive` subcommand name, only after it, so it can't collide with an unrelated subcommand's own `--account` flag.

      --find <TEXT>
          Text to find. Cannot be empty

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --replacement <TEXT>
          Replacement text. Pass an empty string to remove matches

      --range <A1>
          A1 range to search, optionally carrying its own `Sheet!` prefix

      --sheet <NAME>
          Sheet (tab) title. Supplies the prefix for a bare `--range`, and is required with `--whole-sheet`

      --whole-sheet
          Search the whole sheet named by `--sheet`

      --all-sheets
          Search every sheet in the workbook

      --match-case
          Match case exactly

      --match-entire-cell
          Require the search text to match the whole cell

      --search-by-regex
          Interpret the search and replacement as Java regular-expression text

      --include-formulas
          Include cells containing formulas. Without this flag, formula cells are skipped; Sheets has no formulas-only mode

      --dry-run
          Reports the gate verdict and request scope without calling `spreadsheets.batchUpdate`. Sheets computes matching counts only when the request executes

      --lease <TOKEN>
          The lease token from `drive lease acquire`, required unless the deciding write-permission rule sets `require_lease: false` — a token presented anyway is still validated and consumed (ADR-0080 §1/§9/§13). Never needed with `--dry-run`

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi drive sheets create =====
Creates a new Google Sheet, optionally seeded with values. Gated by the folder write-permission rules' `create` operation (issue rust-works/omni-dev#1589)

Usage: gwi drive sheets create [OPTIONS] --name <NAME> --parent <FOLDER_ID>

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Drive account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Drive account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066, ADR-0069). Overrides `GWI_DRIVE_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `drive` subtree — not usable before the `drive` subcommand name, only after it, so it can't collide with an unrelated subcommand's own `--account` flag.

      --name <NAME>
          The new spreadsheet's title

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --parent <FOLDER_ID>
          The folder id to create it in — what the write-permission gate is evaluated against

      --values <PATH|->
          Optional initial values, written to `Sheet1!A1` onwards: a local file path, or `-` to read stdin

      --values-format <VALUES_FORMAT>
          How to parse `--values`. `auto` infers from the file extension

          Possible values:
          - auto: Infer from the path's extension: `.json` is JSON, `.tsv` is TSV, everything else — including stdin — is CSV
          - csv:  RFC 4180 CSV
          - tsv:  Tab-separated values, using CSV quoting rules
          - json: A JSON array of arrays
          
          [default: auto]

      --input <INPUT>
          How the API interprets the values (see `drive sheets write`)

          Possible values:
          - user-entered: Parse values as if typed into the UI: `=SUM(A1:A3)` becomes a formula, `2026-09-06` a date, `1,234` a number
          - raw:          Store every value verbatim as text; a leading `=` stays literal
          
          [default: user-entered]

      --dry-run
          Reports the gate verdict without creating anything

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi drive sheets add-sheet =====
Adds a new sheet (tab) to a spreadsheet. Gated by the folder write-permission rules' `sheets-structure` operation (issue rust-works/omni-dev#1613)

Usage: gwi drive sheets add-sheet [OPTIONS] --title <TITLE> <SPREADSHEET_ID>

Arguments:
  <SPREADSHEET_ID>
          Spreadsheet id (the /d/<ID>/ segment of a Sheets URL).

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Drive account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Drive account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066, ADR-0069). Overrides `GWI_DRIVE_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `drive` subtree — not usable before the `drive` subcommand name, only after it, so it can't collide with an unrelated subcommand's own `--account` flag.

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --title <TITLE>
          Title for the new sheet. Must not already exist in the workbook

      --index <N>
          Zero-based position in the workbook. Omitted appends to the end

      --rows <N>
          Initial row count. Omitted takes Sheets' own default (1000)

      --columns <N>
          Initial column count. Omitted takes Sheets' own default (26)

      --dry-run
          Reports the gate verdict and the change that would be made, without calling `spreadsheets.batchUpdate`

      --lease <TOKEN>
          The lease token from `drive lease acquire`, required unless the deciding write-permission rule sets `require_lease: false` — a token presented anyway is still validated and consumed (ADR-0080 §1/§9/§13). Never needed with `--dry-run`

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
