      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Drive account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Drive account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066, ADR-0069). Overrides `GWI_DRIVE_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `drive` subtree — not usable before the `drive` subcommand name, only after it, so it can't collide with an unrelated subcommand's own `--account` flag.

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --slicer-id <ID>
          Which slicer to move, discovered via `list-slicers`

      --sheet <NAME>
          Sheet title, supplying the prefix for `--anchor` when it doesn't carry its own. Has no effect without `--anchor`

      --anchor <A1>
          The new anchor cell, e.g. `F2`

      --offset-x <PX>
          Additional horizontal offset from the anchor cell, in pixels

      --offset-y <PX>
          Additional vertical offset from the anchor cell, in pixels

      --width <PX>
          The slicer's new width in pixels

      --height <PX>
          The slicer's new height in pixels

      --dry-run
          Reports the gate verdict and the change that would be made, without calling `spreadsheets.batchUpdate`

      --lease <TOKEN>
          The lease token from `drive lease acquire`, required unless the deciding write-permission rule sets `require_lease: false` — a token presented anyway is still validated and consumed (ADR-0080 §1/§9/§13). Never needed with `--dry-run`

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi drive sheets update-chart-border =====
Sets or clears an existing chart's border colour (`updateEmbeddedObjectBorder`). Same gate as `move-chart` (issue rust-works/omni-dev#1837, ADR-0081 §3). Charts only — a slicer has no border

Usage: gwi drive sheets update-chart-border [OPTIONS] --chart-id <ID> <SPREADSHEET_ID>

Arguments:
  <SPREADSHEET_ID>
          Spreadsheet id (the /d/<ID>/ segment of a Sheets URL).

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Drive account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Drive account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066, ADR-0069). Overrides `GWI_DRIVE_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `drive` subtree — not usable before the `drive` subcommand name, only after it, so it can't collide with an unrelated subcommand's own `--account` flag.

      --chart-id <ID>
          Which chart to update, discovered via `list-charts`

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --color <#RRGGBB>
          The new border colour, e.g. `#4A86E8`

      --clear
          Remove the chart's border entirely

      --dry-run
          Reports the gate verdict and the change that would be made, without calling `spreadsheets.batchUpdate`

      --lease <TOKEN>
          The lease token from `drive lease acquire`, required unless the deciding write-permission rule sets `require_lease: false` — a token presented anyway is still validated and consumed (ADR-0080 §1/§9/§13). Never needed with `--dry-run`

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi drive sheets add-pivot-table =====
Writes a new pivot table at an anchor cell. Gated by **both** the folder write-permission rules' `sheets-write` and `sheets-structure` operations (issue rust-works/omni-dev#1798, ADR-0081 §5)

Usage: gwi drive sheets add-pivot-table [OPTIONS] --sheet <NAME> --anchor <A1> --source <[SHEET!]A1_RANGE> --value <COLUMN:FUNC> <SPREADSHEET_ID>

Arguments:
  <SPREADSHEET_ID>
          Spreadsheet id (the /d/<ID>/ segment of a Sheets URL).

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Drive account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Drive account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066, ADR-0069). Overrides `GWI_DRIVE_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `drive` subtree — not usable before the `drive` subcommand name, only after it, so it can't collide with an unrelated subcommand's own `--account` flag.

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --sheet <NAME>
          Sheet (tab) title `--anchor` lives on

      --anchor <A1>
          A single-cell A1 reference (no `Sheet!` prefix — `--sheet` supplies it) the pivot table is anchored at

      --source <[SHEET!]A1_RANGE>
          The pivot's source range. May carry its own `Sheet!` prefix (a pivot commonly sources from a different tab than it's anchored on); falls back to `--sheet` when it doesn't. Must be a bounded rectangle

      --row <COLUMN[:asc|desc]>
          A row grouping, by 0-based column offset into `--source`, optionally followed by a sort order (default `asc`). Repeatable, outermost first

      --column <COLUMN[:asc|desc]>
          A column grouping, by 0-based column offset into `--source`, optionally followed by a sort order (default `asc`). Repeatable, outermost first

      --value <COLUMN:FUNC>
          An aggregated value column, by 0-based column offset into `--source` and a summarize function (`sum`, `counta`, `count`, `countunique`, `average`, `max`, `min`, `median`, `product`, `stdev`, `stdevp`, `var`, `varp`). Repeatable; at least one is required

      --filter <COLUMN:VALUE[,VALUE...]>
          A source-row filter, by 0-based column offset into `--source` and a comma-separated allow-list of raw values. Repeatable

      --value-layout <VALUE_LAYOUT>
          Lays values out as columns (the default) or rows

          Possible values:
          - horizontal: Values laid out as columns
          - vertical:   Values laid out as rows

      --no-totals
          Omits the totals row/column that every group shows by default

      --dry-run
          Reports the gate verdict, the pivot configuration, and the anchor's current content, without calling `spreadsheets.batchUpdate`. Cannot report the region the pivot table will actually overwrite — see `docs/drive.md`

      --lease <TOKEN>
          The lease token from `drive lease acquire`, required unless the deciding write-permission rule sets `require_lease: false` — a token presented anyway is still validated and consumed (ADR-0080 §1/§9/§13). Never needed with `--dry-run`

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

