===== gwi drive sheets text-to-columns =====
Splits a single column's delimited text across the adjacent columns to its right. Gated by the folder `sheets-write` **and** `sheets-structure` operations (issue rust-works/omni-dev#1843, ADR-0083 §§1, 5) — it writes ordinary cell content, and carries the source cell's formatting into the columns it spills into. How many columns the split needs, and the values it writes, can never be previewed; see `text-to-columns --help`

Usage: gwi drive sheets text-to-columns [OPTIONS] --source <A1> --delimiter <DELIMITER> <SPREADSHEET_ID>

Arguments:
  <SPREADSHEET_ID>
          Spreadsheet id (the /d/<ID>/ segment of a Sheets URL).

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Drive account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Drive account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066, ADR-0069). Overrides `GWI_DRIVE_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `drive` subtree — not usable before the `drive` subcommand name, only after it, so it can't collide with an unrelated subcommand's own `--account` flag.

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --sheet <NAME>
          Sheet (tab) title. Supplies the prefix for a bare `--source`

      --source <A1>
          The column to split, optionally carrying its own `Sheet!` prefix. Must resolve to a fully bounded single column (e.g. `A2:A100`)

      --delimiter <DELIMITER>
          Which separator to split on. `auto` lets Sheets detect it itself, including separators this preview cannot try — a tab-separated column splits under `auto` — so an `auto` preview is a guess in both directions. See `--dry-run`
          
          [possible values: comma, semicolon, period, space, auto, custom]

      --custom-delimiter <TEXT>
          The separator text. Required with `--delimiter custom`, and refused with any other `--delimiter`

      --dry-run
          Reports the gate verdict, a local upper-bound split width, and the count and A1 locations of the non-blank cells that would be overwritten — never the values Sheets would write, which cannot be previewed. The width is a true bound for every delimiter but `auto`: Sheets detects separators this preview does not try (a tab-separated column splits under `auto`), so an `auto` preview can under-report as well as over-report, and says so on its own line rather than printing an all-clear

      --lease <TOKEN>
          The lease token from `drive lease acquire`, required unless the deciding write-permission rule sets `require_lease: false` — a token presented anyway is still validated and consumed (ADR-0080 §1/§9/§13). Never needed with `--dry-run`

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi drive sheets trim-whitespace =====
Trims whitespace in every cell of a range, or of a whole sheet. Trimming strips leading and trailing whitespace **and collapses each internal run to a single space** (measured live: `a   b` becomes `a b`); text that trims to something starting `=` or `+` stays a string and is not reinterpreted as a formula. Gated by the folder `sheets-write` operation (issue rust-works/omni-dev#1844, ADR-0083 §1) — it rewrites ordinary cell content in place, doing nothing a `sheets clear` followed by a `sheets write` of the same range could not already do under the same grant. Sheets owns the trim rule, so `--dry-run` reports the non-blank cells that may change, never the ones that will

Usage: gwi drive sheets trim-whitespace [OPTIONS] <SPREADSHEET_ID>

Arguments:
  <SPREADSHEET_ID>
          Spreadsheet id (the /d/<ID>/ segment of a Sheets URL).

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Drive account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Drive account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066, ADR-0069). Overrides `GWI_DRIVE_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `drive` subtree — not usable before the `drive` subcommand name, only after it, so it can't collide with an unrelated subcommand's own `--account` flag.

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --sheet <NAME>
          Sheet (tab) title. Supplies the prefix for a bare `--range`, and is required with `--whole-sheet`

      --range <A1>
          A1 range to trim, optionally carrying its own `Sheet!` prefix (for example `A2:D100`). An open-ended range (`A:A`) is completed from the sheet's current grid extent

      --whole-sheet
          Trim every cell of the sheet named by `--sheet`

      --dry-run
          Reports the gate verdict and the cells that may be trimmed, without calling `spreadsheets.batchUpdate`

      --lease <TOKEN>
          The lease token from `drive lease acquire`, required unless the deciding write-permission rule sets `require_lease: false` — a token presented anyway is still validated and consumed (ADR-0080 §1/§9/§13). Never needed with `--dry-run`

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi drive sheets delete-duplicates =====
Removes duplicate row cells within a bounded range. Gated by the folder `sheets-delete` operation rather than `sheets-write` (issue rust-works/omni-dev#1844, ADR-0083 §2): cells inside the selected range are removed and its survivors shift up. The API selects the rows — keeping the first instance of each duplicate, counting rows that differ only in case, formatting or formulas, and removing filter-hidden rows — so `--dry-run` states that rule instead of listing rows it cannot vouch for. Blank rows duplicate one another, so a range extending past the data can remove every blank row but the first. Columns outside the range stay in place, so a narrow range can misalign records

Usage: gwi drive sheets delete-duplicates [OPTIONS] --range <A1> <SPREADSHEET_ID>

Arguments:
  <SPREADSHEET_ID>
          Spreadsheet id (the /d/<ID>/ segment of a Sheets URL).

Options:
      --account <NAME>
          Selects a named Drive account configured in `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style, mirrors the top-level `--profile`) for this invocation.
          
          Orthogonal to `--profile`: switching the Drive account never changes which profile is active, and vice versa (see ADR-0066, ADR-0069). Overrides `GWI_DRIVE_ACCOUNT`. Scoped to the `drive` subtree — not usable before the `drive` subcommand name, only after it, so it can't collide with an unrelated subcommand's own `--account` flag.

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --sheet <NAME>
          Sheet (tab) title. Supplies the prefix for a bare `--range`

      --range <A1>
          Fully bounded A1 range to dedupe, optionally carrying its own `Sheet!` prefix (for example `A2:D100`)

      --comparison-column <COLUMN>
          An absolute, zero-based sheet column index to compare. Repeat to compare several. Omit to compare every column in the range

      --dry-run
          Reports the gate verdict and the request scope without calling `spreadsheets.batchUpdate`. Which rows are duplicates is computed by Sheets only when the request executes

      --lease <TOKEN>
          The lease token from `drive lease acquire`, required unless the deciding write-permission rule sets `require_lease: false` — a token presented anyway is still validated and consumed (ADR-0080 §1/§9/§13). Never needed with `--dry-run`

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - table: Human-readable table
          - json:  JSON
          - yaml:  YAML (single document)
          - yamls: YAML stream (`---`-separated multi-document)
          - jsonl: JSON Lines: one compact JSON object per line, streaming-friendly
          
          [default: table]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi import =====
Import: copy Gmail and Drive settings and the Drive lease ledger from omni-dev

Usage: gwi import [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --source <PATH>
          The omni-dev settings file to read.
          
          Defaults to `~/.omni-dev/settings.json`. It is only read, never changed.

      --source-ledger <PATH>
          The omni-dev lease ledger to read.
          
          Defaults to `omni-dev/lease-ledger.jsonl` in the state directory. It is only read, never changed; a missing ledger is not an error.

      --dry-run
          Show what would be imported and write nothing.
          
          Conflicts are reported as part of the preview and do not make a dry run fail: it exits 0 unless the import itself fails. A real run exits 1 on a conflict.

      --force
          Overwrite items and leases that gwi already has with a different value.
          
          Without it such items are reported as conflicts and left as they are.

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi log =====
Log: search the request and audit logs, and prune the request log

Usage: gwi log [OPTIONS]
       gwi log <COMMAND>

Commands:
  prune  Prune old records to bound the log's on-disk growth
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --since <DUR_OR_TS>
          Lower time bound: a relative window (`30m`, `2h`, `1d`), a date (`2026-07-01`), or an RFC3339 timestamp

      --until <DUR_OR_TS>
          Upper time bound: same forms as `--since` (a relative value means that long ago). Pair with `--since` for a bounded window

      --method <METHOD>
          Match the HTTP method (case-insensitive), e.g. `GET`

      --status <STATUS>
          Match the status: exact (`200`), class (`5xx`), list (`4xx,5xx`), comparison (`>=400`), or a Drive-mutation status such as `blocked` (exact, case-insensitive; a comma list is accepted). Same values as `status:` in `--query`. A Drive-mutation status that no scanned record has draws a warning on stderr (a likely typo); the exit code is unchanged

      --service <NAME>
          Match the service tag, e.g. `gmail`, `drive`

      --command <PATH>
          Match the resolved command path prefix, e.g. `"gmail read"`

      --url <SUBSTR>
          Match a substring of the request URL

      --grep <REGEX>
          Match a regular expression against the raw JSON line

      --fuzzy <TOKEN>
          Require a fuzzy token (substring of the raw line); repeatable, AND-ed

      --query <EXPR>
          A query expression (AND/OR/NOT, `field:value`, bare tokens); repeatable, AND-ed together. A field that is not built in is looked up in the record's context, and a warning names it if no record has it. Quote a word to search for it as text, e.g. `--query '"not"'`

      --id <ID>
          Match this record `id` or `invocation_id` (pulls a run and its requests)

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format

          Possible values:
          - oneline: One compact line per record (default)
          - json:    The on-disk NDJSON line, verbatim (composes with `jq`)
          - full:    A labelled, multi-line block per record
          
          [default: oneline]

  -n, --limit <N>
          Show at most N (most recent) matching records

      --rotated
          Include numbered request-log backups, oldest first, before the live log. The limit applies across all files; follow then tails only the live log

  -f, --follow
          Follow the log, printing new matching records as they are appended

      --audit
          Read the fail-closed audit log (`audit.jsonl`) instead of the best-effort request log — the leased-write lifecycle and refusal trail (ADR-0080 §11), not subject to `GWI_LOG_DISABLE`, rotation or `prune`. Every filter, the `--query` mini-language and all three output formats apply unchanged; only the file being read differs

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== gwi log prune =====
Prune old records to bound the log's on-disk growth

Usage: gwi log prune [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --older-than <DUR>
          Remove records older than this relative window (e.g. `7d`, `24h`, `2w`)

      --profile <NAME>
          Selects a named credential/config profile from `~/.gwi/settings.json` (AWS-CLI style).
          
          When set, the profile's `env` bundle replaces the base `env` map in the settings-fallback chain (process env still wins); the base map is not consulted. Overrides `GWI_PROFILE`. An unknown name is a hard error listing the known profiles.

      --max-size <SIZE>
          Trim the oldest records until the file is at most this size (e.g. `10mb`, `512kb`, `1048576`); applied after `--older-than`

      --dry-run
          Report what would be removed without modifying the log

      --audit
          Refused outright: the audit log (`audit.jsonl`) is exempt from pruning by design (ADR-0080 §11) — present only so the refusal is explicit rather than a silent no-op or a confusing "no such flag"

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
